custom_dst_smartptr_owning_drop_uaf valgrind 2 tests/logos/pass/custom_dst_smartptr_owning_drop
    # 2026-09-08. THE FIXTURE IS PINNED GREEN OVER A USE-AFTER-FREE. `exit: 42` holds on
    # the base binary and under the Rust-canonical drop landing of this round; NEITHER
    # HALF MOVES IT, in either direction, so it is not evidence for or against them.
    # It is here because it was READ this round, with valgrind, and it reproduces:
    #   Invalid read of size 8 at `__drop_in_place__A`, 16 bytes inside a 64-byte block
    #   free'd by `MyRc$G1$udyn_Tr__drop_me__g__refmut_MyRc$G1$T`
    #   Invalid read of size 8 at `test.A__drop__f__A`, same block
    # i.e. `MyRc::drop_me` FREES the block and the owning-custom-DST drop glue then reads
    # the prefix field and runs `A::drop` through the freed storage. Two invalid reads,
    # exit code 42 both times — the strongest oracle the corpus has (a destructor COUNT)
    # cannot see it either, because the destructor does run, once, on dead memory.
    # ⚠ NOT A SOUNDNESS-QUEUE ROW, and the reason is the queue's own oracle: `observed`
    # is `admits`/`refuses`/`run <rc>`/`diag`, and this program's rc is RIGHT. A row
    # would have to lie to be held. What it owes is either a queue `observed` kind that
    # a memory checker can answer, or a program that makes the same free observable to
    # an exit code (write a sentinel into the block before the free and read it after).
    # A previous round recorded "its recorded UAF is NOT reproduced by either arm". That
    # is CONTRADICTED here: it was not reproduced because it was not run under valgrind.

e0713_mutref_past_destructor_needs_a_new_carrier carrier 2 tests/imported/fail/nll/
    # 2026-09-08. TWO ROWS LOST THEIR CARRIER, NOT THEIR DEFECT. `enum-drop-access` and
    # `issue-52059-report-when-borrow-and-drop-conflict` were rowed for an E0713 hole: a
    # `&mut` handed OUT of a function past a destructor which then writes through it
    # (`DropOpt::drop` stores 9 through `self.r`, the caller then stores 5 through the
    # returned `&mut`). Both programs express it the same way — `fn f(x: DropStruct) ->
    # &mut T { return x.field; }` — and the E0509 landing of 2026-09-08 refuses exactly
    # that, correctly: rustc refuses it as E0509 too, before E0713 is ever consulted.
    # ⚠ SO THE PROGRAMS ARE ILLEGAL AND THE ROWS COULD NOT STAND, BUT THE HOLE IS OPEN AND
    # IS NOW UNOBSERVED. Nothing in the tree tests whether a `&mut` that outlives a
    # destructor is caught. What this owes is a CARRIER: a legal-but-for-E0713 program
    # that gets the reference out without moving a field out of a `Drop` value — e.g. a
    # `&mut self` method returning a reborrow (`fn take(&mut self) -> &mut i64 { &mut
    # *self.r }`) whose result is used after the owner is dropped, or a Drop-less wrapper
    # holding the Drop value. Write it, check whether logosc admits it, and row it.
    # ⚠ IT IS ALSO A LESSON ABOUT COVERAGE: a refusal landed for one rule DELETED the
    # only two observations of another. A row's program is its instrument, and a stronger
    # rule upstream of it can silently retire the instrument while leaving the defect.

dyn_vtable_bare_key_miscompile  defect     2  src/compiler/mlir_gen_dyn.cpp:962,982
